<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Levedi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Levedi was an early leader of the Hungarians, described by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> as the first of their voivodes when they lived under the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>. The Khazar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> gave him a noble Khazar wife and offered to make him prince of the Hungarians. He declined in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos">Álmos</a> or his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>, the founder of the ruling dynasty.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His name appears as Lebed, Levedias, Lebedias and Lebedi, and the only source for his life is the De administrando imperio, written by the Byzantine emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus around 950. The origin of the name is disputed: some derive it from the Slavic word for swan, Omeljan Pritsak from a Turkic title meaning brave lord, while Gyula Kristó and other scholars favour a Finno-Ugric root linked to the old Hungarian verb lesz, to be. A similar name, Lewedi, appears in a Hungarian charter of 1138. His title of voivode is Slavic, and Constantine used it for the heads of the seven Hungarian tribes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to Constantine, the Hungarians lived beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and fought as their allies in all their wars, and for this the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> gave Levedi a noble Khazar lady in marriage, though not one of his own family, so that she might bear him children; the marriage was childless. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, driven out by the Khazars, defeated the Hungarians, part of them went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and the rest moved west with Levedi.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> then summoned him and offered to make him prince so that he would obey the khagan's word. Levedi answered that he was not strong enough for this rule, and the Hungarians raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a> on a shield in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> manner. The land of Levedia was named after him, although some have argued that the chieftain took his name from the land, which Kristó rejects as contrary to the source and to Hungarian naming practice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Hungarian historians have debated him for two centuries. Antal Bartha thought him a mythical figure, and Tamás Hölbling a phantom invented from the place name. Several scholars, among them Bálint Hóman and György Györffy, identified him with Előd, the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos">Álmos</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-of-keza">Simon of Kéza</a>'s chronicle, a link the linguist Katalin Fehértói rejected. Historians also differ over when he lived and what office he held.</p><p>Kristó placed his birth around 800 and his leadership in Levedia from about 830 to about 850, while Györffy had him head the tribes as kende between 870 and 893. Balázs Sudár argued that the marriage shows he ranked below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> and was not head of all the Hungarians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Levedi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constantine VII describes seven tribes led first by <b>Levedi</b>, under the authority of the Khazar Khaganate.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-tribal-federation">Hungarian tribal federation</a></li></ul></section>
